WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.300
Across the nation, proof that education
reforms are working has come from record

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.370
graduation rates. But in DC, the
unbelievable progress is just that. After

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:20.250
posting a record 73% grad rate last year,
an audit found one in three DC grads

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.349
received diplomas in violation of policy.
Excessive absences were one problem:

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.970
nearly a third of graduates missed 50
days in their final year. Another was

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.660
credit recovery, which offered easy
makeup classes to students who never

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.520
took the original courses. All told, if DC
had followed policy, its grad rate would

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.899
have fallen below 50%. So is DC an
outlier or the canary in the coal mine?

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:47.250
Well, under similar pressure for results,
grad rate issues surfaced in Maryland,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.320
Chicago, LA, New York, and elsewhere. Look, everyone wants more students to graduate.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.219
These shortcuts aren't the answer. They
make it hard for us to tell which

00:00:56.219 --> 00:01:00.210
districts are actually pushing more
students to earn diplomas. DC is a lesson

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.050
that we can't improve the lives of
students or school systems unless school

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:08.420
leaders push our students to new heights,
rather than just pretending to.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.700
How do you think school districts can make real
progress on graduation rates? Let us know

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.880
in your comments. Also, let us know what
other topics you'd like our scholars to

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.409
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like
and subscribe for more research and

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:25.579
videos from AEI.

